# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# openclaw EGRESS GATEWAY — v4.1 Phase 1c (LAURA-I1-03 close, compose side)
# Last updated: 2026-07-06
#
# SINGLE SOURCE. This file is imported (top-level) by every Caddyfile variant
#   docker/Caddyfile.{selfsigned,ca,acme}   via  import /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.openclaw-egress
# and rendered as a hand-coded K8s variant into the Helm caddy ConfigMap
#   helm/yashigani/templates/configmaps.yaml (:18790 block, gated on
#   .Values.agentBundles.openclaw.enabled) — no compose/helm drift (Iris parity
#   rule; retro #81-b: helm render substitutes yashigani-* service names +
#   ca_bundle.crt trust anchor; THIS file is the design source of truth).
#
# WHY (LAURA-I1-03, High/Bypass, proven live): openclaw was dual-homed on the
# `edge` bridge (internal:false) with arbitrary internet egress while holding
# YASHIGANI_INTERNAL_BEARER — a one-hop exfiltration path. Fix: openclaw leaves
# `edge` entirely; its ONLY outbound path to the internet is THIS gateway.
#
# DESIGN (Laura threat model laura-forwardproxy-threatmodel.md, GO-WITH-FIXES):
#   FP-01 — this is a FIXED-UPSTREAM reverse_proxy gateway, NOT a CONNECT/open
#           forward proxy. The destination set is the three literal upstreams
#           below. openclaw never supplies a destination host — the entire
#           open-relay / SSRF / DNS-rebind / IP-literal / domain-fronting class
#           has no input field to attack. caddyserver/forwardproxy is
#           deliberately NOT used (NO-GO condition).
#   FP-02 — the allowlist IS this file (perimeter-owned, single-source render).
#           No destination host exists in openclaw.json / openclaw env — a
#           compromised openclaw cannot rewrite its own destination set.
#           Enforced by tests/invariants/test_openclaw_egress_gateway.py.
#   FP-03 — caller auth = openclaw's per-instance mesh leaf. The listener
#           requires_and_verifies a leaf from the internal intermediate CA AND
#           each route hard-matches the URI SAN against openclaw's SPIFFE ID
#           (parse-time env {$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SPIFFE_ID}; compose composes it
#           from the per-instance trust domain). Any other mesh peer — even one
#           holding a valid leaf (e.g. gateway) — is refused 403. The listener
#           additionally rides a dedicated 2-member bridge (openclaw_ringfence:
#           openclaw + caddy ONLY, internal:true) so no other container can even
#           dial :18790. NOT bearer-auth (FP-08: the baked bearer is exactly
#           what an openclaw attacker holds).
#   FP-04 — moot by construction: Caddy resolves the fixed hosts itself, dials
#           :443 only, verifies the upstream cert against the system (public)
#           CA pool [transport tls default], and pins SNI via tls_server_name.
#   FP-07 — hooks.slack.com (Slack response_url callbacks) and api.telegram.org
#           (bot API + /file/bot<token>/... downloads) are IN the allowlist as
#           exact FQDNs (no wildcards), so those legitimate callbacks flow
#           through THIS gateway — no second unfiltered egress path exists
#           (openclaw has no other route to the internet at L3).
#   FP-06 — RESIDUAL (registered with Lu, Phase 2): a host allowlist cannot
#           stop exfil to an ATTACKER-OWNED Slack workspace / Telegram bot —
#           both are legitimate slack.com / api.telegram.org destinations.
#           Compensating controls owed in Phase 2: bot-token workspace/chat
#           pinning, M4/DLP content filter on egress bodies, outbound
#           rate-limiting, per-request audit. Do NOT treat this gateway as a
#           data-loss control; it is a DESTINATION control.
#
# Defence in depth: caddy-entrypoint.sh mirrors these three FQDNs in the
# container-level iptables OUTPUT allowlist (YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_EGRESS=1,
# set by install.sh when the openclaw profile is enabled). The Caddyfile
# literals are the PRIMARY control; iptables is belt-and-suspenders.
#
# openclaw-side wiring — COMPLETE (v4.1 Phase 1d, NO-CARRY-FORWARD):
# openclaw's Slack/Telegram SDK calls are configured to route through:
#   https://caddy:18790/slack        (Slack Web API  → slack.com)
#   https://caddy:18790/slack-hooks  (response_url    → hooks.slack.com)
#   https://caddy:18790/telegram     (Bot API + files → api.telegram.org)
# via TWO wired SDK mechanisms:
#   (a) openclaw.json egress block (certFile/keyFile + apiBaseUrl per channel)
#       read by the binary via OPENCLAW_CONFIG_PATH=/etc/openclaw/openclaw.json
#   (b) OPENCLAW_EGRESS_TLS_{CERT,KEY}_FILE env vars (belt-and-suspenders)
# Trust anchor: NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/etc/openclaw/tls/ca.crt (server cert).
#
# Static proof (cert chain → SAN gate):
#   /etc/openclaw/tls/client.crt (./secrets/openclaw_client.crt)
#   URI SAN: spiffe://yashigani.internal/openclaw (service_identities.yaml:248)
#   :18790 CEL gate: {san.uris.0} != spiffe://yashigani.internal/openclaw → 403
#   Match: cert SAN satisfies the gate → require_and_verify accepts it.
# Nothing deferred. Laura Phase-3 test (c) is live confirmation of this chain.
#
# Port 18790 (front) is distinct from openclaw's own 18789, the Ollama front
# 11435, the mesh listeners 8444/8445, and the codegen MCP mesh-port range
# 9500-9900; never host-mapped (no compose `ports:` entry).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Caller gate — exact SPIFFE SAN match on the VERIFIED peer certificate.
# require_and_verify (below) already guarantees the cert chains to the internal
# intermediate CA; this snippet narrows "any mesh member" to "openclaw only".
# Parse-time env substitution: if YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SPIFFE_ID is unset the
# default literal applies; an empty substitution would fail CLOSED (no leaf
# carries an empty URI SAN, so the != matcher fires and 403s everything).
(openclaw-egress-caller-gate) {
    @caller_not_openclaw expression {http.request.tls.client.san.uris.0} != "{$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SPIFFE_ID:spiffe://yashigani.internal/openclaw}"
    respond @caller_not_openclaw "Forbidden" 403
}

:18790 {
    tls /run/secrets/caddy_client.crt /run/secrets/caddy_client.key {
        client_auth {
            mode require_and_verify
            trust_pool file /run/secrets/ca_intermediate.crt
        }
        protocols tls1.3
        ciphers TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256
        curves x25519mlkem768 x25519 secp384r1 secp256r1
    }

    # FP-06 Phase 2 — egress audit log (every outbound request, JSON, stderr).
    # Fires on ALL requests to :18790 — including 403 denies from the caller gate
    # and the bot-ID pin below; denied requests are the most audit-relevant events.
    # Captures: ts, method, URI (upstream-facing after handle_path strip), status,
    # response bytes, duration, TLS client common name (openclaw leaf). The
    # Authorization header (openclaw's bearer tokens to Slack/Telegram) is redacted
    # by the filter encoder — it must NOT appear in cleartext in the log stream.
    log {
        output stderr
        format filter {
            wrap json
            fields {
                request>headers>Authorization delete
            }
        }
    }

    # Outbound body cap — light exfil-volume brake (primary volume/DLP control
    # is FP-06 Phase 2). Matches the public edge cap (OWASP ASVS 4.1.7).
    request_body {
        max_size 10MB
    }

    # ── Slack Web API — https://slack.com/api/... ────────────────────────────
    # openclaw dials /slack/api/chat.postMessage → upstream /api/chat.postMessage
    handle_path /slack/* {
        route {
            import openclaw-egress-caller-gate
            # FP-06 Phase 2 — INERT Slack workspace-pin hook.
            # The robust form (workspace pinning via token injection) requires a
            # per-install YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN secret provisioned by
            # install.sh. No such secret exists today (not in openclaw.json, compose,
            # or install.sh). Provisioning is gated by the suggest-yes-create-never
            # invariant and needs an explicit Tiago greenlight.
            # When provisioned, activate by uncommenting these two lines:
            #   header_up -Authorization
            #   header_up Authorization "Bearer {$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN}"
            # DO NOT wire live with an empty token: an empty inject breaks all Slack
            # egress. Install path: install.sh → /run/secrets/openclaw_slack_bot_token
            # → {$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN} parse-time env substitution.
            reverse_proxy https://slack.com {
                # Pin Host + SNI to the allowlisted FQDN (anti domain-fronting,
                # FP-04e). Upstream cert verified against the public CA pool
                # (Caddy transport tls default — no tls_trust_pool override).
                header_up Host slack.com
                # Never leak mesh identity material upstream.
                header_up -X-SPIFFE-ID
                header_up -X-Caddy-Verified-Secret
                transport http {
                    tls
                    tls_server_name slack.com
                }
            }
        }
    }

    # ── Slack response_url callbacks — https://hooks.slack.com/... (FP-07) ──
    handle_path /slack-hooks/* {
        route {
            import openclaw-egress-caller-gate
            # FP-06 Phase 2 — INERT Slack workspace-pin hook (same as /slack/*).
            # Awaits Tiago-greenlit provisioning of YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN.
            #   header_up -Authorization
            #   header_up Authorization "Bearer {$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN}"
            reverse_proxy https://hooks.slack.com {
                header_up Host hooks.slack.com
                header_up -X-SPIFFE-ID
                header_up -X-Caddy-Verified-Secret
                transport http {
                    tls
                    tls_server_name hooks.slack.com
                }
            }
        }
    }

    # ── Telegram Bot API + file downloads — https://api.telegram.org/... ────
    # Covers both /bot<token>/<method> and /file/bot<token>/<path> (FP-07).
    handle_path /telegram/* {
        route {
            import openclaw-egress-caller-gate
            # FP-06 Phase 2 — Telegram bot-ID pin (destination-account control).
            # Env: YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_TELEGRAM_BOT_ID (parse-time, default empty string).
            # After handle_path strips /telegram, Telegram API paths take the form:
            #   /bot<botid>:<token>/<method>
            # Enforcement: when the pin is NON-EMPTY, only paths whose prefix matches
            # /bot{pin}: pass; all others → 403. This pins the egress to one specific
            # Telegram bot account — an attacker-controlled bot on the same api.telegram.org
            # origin is rejected at the path-level even though the host allowlist passes it.
            # Inert-when-empty: the first clause ("<pin>" != "") is false when the env var
            # is unset, so the entire expression short-circuits to false → matcher never fires
            # → current behaviour (no enforcement) is preserved on a default install.
            # Verified: caddy adapt compiles cleanly with pin unset (empty string).
            @wrong_bot_id expression "{$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_TELEGRAM_BOT_ID:}" != "" && !{http.request.uri.path}.startsWith("/bot{$YASHIGANI_OPENCLAW_TELEGRAM_BOT_ID:}:")
            respond @wrong_bot_id "Forbidden" 403
            reverse_proxy https://api.telegram.org {
                header_up Host api.telegram.org
                header_up -X-SPIFFE-ID
                header_up -X-Caddy-Verified-Secret
                transport http {
                    tls
                    tls_server_name api.telegram.org
                }
            }
        }
    }

    # Default-deny: no other destination exists on this listener.
    handle {
        respond "Forbidden" 403
    }
}
